/target/
**/target/

# Host build settings: where cargo puts artifacts on THIS machine. Both files pin
# target-dir to a local-disk path that exists on the development workstation and nowhere
# else, and on Linux or macOS cargo reads an absolute Windows path as a relative one, so a
# clone would build into a literal "C:" directory inside itself. They are kept on the
# working tree - the NAS copy is a robocopy mirror, so the pin travels with it and a cargo
# run launched against the share still lands off it - and out of the published repository,
# where a clone builds into its own target/ like any other Rust project.
# tools/ci/check-target-dir.sh is the tripwire that does belong in the repository.
# firmware/.cargo/config.toml stays tracked: it carries the target triple, the linker and
# the IDF pins, and holds no machine-specific path.
/.cargo/config.toml
/tools/xverify/.cargo/config.toml

# Release artifacts from the container build (tools/repro/build.sh writes /out,
# check-repro.sh writes out/check-repro/). They are published from a release page,
# never committed: a binary in git is a second copy of the thing the whole
# reproducibility exercise exists to make unnecessary.
/out/

# Enclosure models are kept out of the published repo until their provenance and
# licence are settled - third-party models (e.g. Makerworld) carry their own terms
# that may not permit redistribution under GPL-3.0. Revisit for 0.2.0.
/3dp/

# The original internal project brief. Kept on the working tree for provenance but
# out of the published repository: it records local working paths and internal
# workflow instructions that mean nothing to a reader of the public tree. docs/
# carries the design record that superseded it.
/plan.md

# Python bytecode, from running tools/repro/verify-manifest.py.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

**/*.rs.bk
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Host operations tooling. tools/ops is about the DEVELOPMENT WORKSTATION - a NAS mirror,
# a kernel-pool watcher, a handle-leak hunt - not about the device, the firmware or the
# release. Every script in it hardcodes this machine's UNC paths and addresses and does
# nothing for anyone who clones the repository. docs/OPS-HOST.md is the same material in
# prose. Both are kept on the working tree, and out of the published one.
/tools/ops/
/docs/OPS-HOST.md

# The mirror hook, for the same reason: it fires tools/ops/mirror-to-nas.ps1, which does
# not exist in a clone. The other two hooks in that directory are repository policy and
# are tracked.
/tools/git-hooks/post-commit

# poolwatch.ps1 output: kernel pool samples from this host, rewritten on every boot.
/poolwatch/

# Superseded draft, replaced by docs/VERIFYING.md, which is what tools/release.sh and
# tools/ci/check-repro-pins.sh both name. Kept on the working tree until its removal is
# confirmed, and out of the repository meanwhile: it teaches a signature check that does
# not pin the key, which is the one mistake a verifier cannot afford.
/VERIFYING.md

# A point-in-time readiness snapshot of this workstation's tree: it records gate results
# that are already superseded, and local build paths that mean nothing in a clone. Kept on
# the working tree; publishing it is the owner's call, not this tree's default.
/docs/release-readiness-0.2.0.md
